    "slug": "vector-contract",
    "title": "Use column operations without changing business meaning",
    "intro": "A retailer calculates invoice net values from millions of lines. Column-oriented arithmetic can remove Python loop overhead, but a faster expression is useful only if units and missing-value rules stay correct.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Express the transformation as aligned columns",
        "A pandas Series is a labelled column, and arithmetic between Series aligns labels rather than blindly pairing physical row positions. Vectorized arithmetic expresses a column operation through array-oriented implementations, often avoiding Python work per row. It does not guarantee that every expression uses little memory or that every string operation is faster. Measure the real workload after choosing a correct representation.",
        "Define units and validity before computing. Integer cents avoid binary floating-point rounding in simple exact monetary addition, but percentages and currency conversion still require an explicit rounding rule. Missing amounts may mean unknown, not zero. Treating an absent refund as zero can be valid only if the source contract distinguishes no refund from missing refund information. Preserve these decisions in transformation documentation."
      ],
      [
        "Select before copying and validate the result",
        "Project needed columns and use a boolean validity mask before arithmetic when the contract rejects malformed rows. Explicit copies at ownership boundaries make mutation intent clear across pandas versions; do not rely on a chained assignment having a particular effect. Record rejected-row counts and reasons rather than silently dropping input that affects business totals.",
        "The fixture computes net cents for rows whose amount and refund are known, then reports the excluded count. The calculation is real pandas execution over nullable integers; it is not a benchmark and does not prove production-scale memory efficiency. A performance review should measure elapsed time and peak memory on representative data while preserving the same null, index and rounding semantics."
      ]
    ],
    "code": "import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({'cents':pd.array([1000,700,None],dtype='Int64'),'refund':pd.array([100,0,50],dtype='Int64')})\nvalid=df['cents'].notna() & df['refund'].notna()\nclean=df.loc[valid,['cents','refund']].copy()\nclean['net']=clean['cents']-clean['refund']\nprint(clean['net'].tolist()); print(int((~valid).sum()))",
    "expect": "[900, 700]\n1",
    "problem": "Calculate valid invoice net amounts and account for excluded records.",
