    "practice": "What should a transformation assertion encode?|An independent business invariant|A copy of every implementation step|Only the current output file name|Only the task execution duration|A useful assertion compares behavior with a separately reasoned contract rather than repeating the code under test.\nWhat does a unit fixture primarily isolate?|A bounded logic behavior|Every warehouse permission rule|All production input distributions|Every source-system outage|A small fixture validates specific behavior under stated inputs and assumptions.\nWhy review expected-value changes in a pull request?|They may redefine the contract|They always fix source defects|They replace all integration tests|They guarantee faster execution|Updated expectations can legitimize a semantic change or merely conceal a regression, so their rationale matters.\nA cancelled shipment starts contributing after a filter refactor. Which fixture directly detects the defect?|A cancelled-only contribution case|Only a large valid-shipment sample|Only a schema column-name check|Only a successful query parse test|A cancelled-only case isolates the required exclusion and should produce zero under the declared rule.\nA change affects a shared settlement model used by six marts. Which test selection is safer?|Affected model and downstream contracts|Only the edited source file parser|Only a random unrelated smoke check|Only the first mart alphabetically|Dependency-aware testing must follow semantic impact to consumers rather than stop at the edited file.\nA test computes expected revenue using the same predicate as production. What risk remains?|A shared predicate bug can pass|Every output must become null|The source necessarily loses rows|The database cannot create indexes|Duplicating logic in the oracle can reproduce the defect rather than detect it.\nA green local suite uses SQLite while production SQL uses a warehouse-specific merge syntax. What evidence is still needed?|Target-engine integration checks|Only more identical Python fixtures|Only the repository commit count|Only a larger output row limit|Local logic evidence does not establish compatibility with an unexecuted engine-specific statement.",
    "formal": "What is an independent expected result useful for?|Detecting shared logic mistakes|Replacing every runtime check|Proving all inputs are valid|Guaranteeing zero source latency|An oracle reasoned separately from the implementation can reveal defects that duplicated logic would repeat.\nWhich input checks exclusion behavior most directly?|A record that should be excluded|Only a normal accepted record|Only the model\u2019s schema file|Only the scheduler run label|A boundary example must exercise the condition that the rule excludes.\nWhat does a reconciliation add beyond syntax validation?|A comparison with an external control|A guarantee of business approval|An automatic access permission grant|A substitute for all key tests|Reconciliation checks an independently defined measure or population rather than merely statement validity.\nA pull request changes both a transformation and every failed expected output without explanation. What should the reviewer request?|Rationale and contract cases|Only a faster test runner setting|Only fewer fixture records per test|Only a renamed model output column|Expected values must follow a justified contract, not simply the implementation\u2019s new output.\nA pipeline handles empty input by emitting the previous day\u2019s rows. Policy says no data means an empty new result. Which test is missing?|An explicit empty-input contract case|Only a larger valid-data fixture|Only a source schema existence check|Only a timestamp serialization check|Empty input has distinct semantics that ordinary populated fixtures may never exercise.\nA source model\u2019s null policy changes but its columns remain identical. Which consumers may need checks?|All lineage-dependent aggregations|Only tests of directly edited models|Only tests with changed output column names|Only downstream dashboards that failed before|Semantic changes propagate through dependencies even when output schema names stay stable.|Semantic dependencies extend beyond the edited file or its local implementation tests.|A null-policy change can alter measures without renaming columns.|A previously passing dashboard may still inherit a new semantic regression.\nA valid-data fixture passes but a duplicated key inflates revenue in production. Which independent test best targets the missing contract?|Duplicate-key contribution behavior|More accepted rows without duplicate identities|Only a successful result schema assertion|Only a faster repeated sum benchmark|A fixture must include the multiplicity failure mode rather than assume accepted rows are unique.|Larger normal fixtures do not exercise the multiplicity failure mode.|Schema validity does not verify how many times each business key contributes.|Execution speed does not detect double-counting caused by repeated source keys.\nA developer removes a failing reconciliation because a unit test already checks the sum function. What evidence is lost?|Source population reconciliation|Only source column-type evidence|Only expected sum function arithmetic|Only the successful warehouse connection log|A unit sum check does not establish that the pipeline read the correct population or reconciled it with authoritative totals.|Types do not establish the source population or its independent control total.|A correct sum function can still aggregate the wrong or incomplete population.|Connection success does not establish data completeness or reconciliation.\nA data contract deliberately redefines chargebacks to apply on settlement day. Existing historical fixtures change. Which review package is appropriate?|Business rationale plus before/after affected cases|Accept snapshots whenever the new query passes|Keep old expectations and hide the rule change|Rewrite every historical source row in fixtures|A legitimate semantic revision still needs independently explained cases and its downstream/historical impact.|A passing implementation is not an independent justification for revised business meaning.|Concealing a deliberate contract change preserves misleading expectations and comparisons.|Source fixtures should retain evidence; the transformation and expected contract should explain changed attribution.\nA pipeline passes unit fixtures and schema checks, but its target-engine merge writes a cursor before data commit. The reviewer proposes adding more null-input unit cases. What additional test addresses the real failure boundary?|Crash/restart commit coherence|More null-value pure-function unit tests|Repeated successful target-engine compilation|Additional source type and range assertions|The suspected fault crosses persistence boundaries, so it requires integration failure/recovery evidence; unrelated unit examples cannot prove commit coherence.|The suspected defect is persistence ordering, not the pure function\u2019s null behavior.|Compilation does not execute a crash between cursor and data commits.|Source quality assertions do not prove output/cursor crash consistency.",
